['In C programming, you can use the standard library's `rand()` function to generate a pseudo-random number and `srand(time(NULL))` for different seed every time you run your program for more randomness. Here is a simple solution:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    srand(time(NULL)); // seed the pseudo-random number generator with current time

    int num1 = rand() % 990 + 10; // generate a random integer between 10 and 1000
    printf("The first random number is: %d\n", num1);

    int num2 = rand() % 990 + 10; // generate another random integer between 10 and 1000
    printf("The second random number is: %d\n", num2);

    int difference = abs(num1 - num2); // calculate the absolute difference of these two numbers
    printf("The difference between the two integers is: %d\n", difference);

    return 0;
}
```

This program generates two random numbers, calculates their absolute difference and prints them out. Note that it's important to seed `rand()` with a different value each time you run your program, otherwise `rand()` will always produce the same sequence of pseudo-random numbers. This is why we use `srand(time(NULL))` to generate a different seed every second, making it more "random".
']

