    printf("The string is: \"%s\"\n", buffer);

    return 0;
}
```

Please note that this code doesn't actually use `scanf` in the way you described (i.e., reading a string until it sees a newline character). It reads characters one by one, checking if each is a newline or not. If it's not, it adds it to a buffer and increments an index; otherwise, it just skips over it. When we've read enough non-newline characters (i.e., when our counter hits 1023), we stop reading and add a null terminator to the end of our string.
