['Here's a simple implementation in C that does what you described. This version uses `system()` function to execute shell commands and checks the return value, which corresponds to the status of the process it created.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int run_command(char* username) {
    char command[50];
    sprintf(command, "id %s", username);
    return system(command);
}

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: ./program <username>\n");
        return 1;
    }

    int status = run_command(argv[1]);

    printf("Exit status of the command is %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));

    if (WIFEXITED(status)) {
        printf("The id command has finished with exit code %d\n", WEXITSTATUS(status));
    } else {
        printf("The id command did not finish properly\n");
    }

    return 0;
}
```
This program checks the number of arguments provided. If it's not exactly two (the program name and one additional argument), it prints an error message and exits with a status code indicating failure.

Otherwise, it calls `run_command()` with the second argument as its parameter. This function uses `sprintf()` to construct the command string using the provided username, then runs that command via `system()`. 

The return value from `system()` is a system-dependent status code representing how and why the process terminated. On POSIX systems, this code includes a termination signal if the child was terminated by a signal and the low byte of the status contains the signal number that killed the process. If the child exited normally, the high byte of the status will be zero. 

This value is then printed out to standard output. `WEXITSTATUS()` function is used to get just the exit status from this code.

The return value from the main function is always successful (zero), even if running the command failed or was not successful, as per your original requirements. If you want to change that so the program returns a non-zero value in case of failure, you can add some logic based on `WIFEXITED(status)` and `WEXITSTATUS(status)` values.
']

