['Sure, here's an example C code that accomplishes what you asked for using `system()` to execute the ls command and `printf()` for printing:

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void executeCommand(const char *format, ...) {
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, format);

    // Allocate enough memory for the command string
    int size = vsnprintf(NULL, 0, format, args) + 1;
    if (size < 0) {
        perror("vsnprintf");
        return;
    }
    char *command = malloc(size);

    // Create the command string using the arguments
    vsnprintf(command, size, format, args);
    va_end(args);

    printf("\nExecuting: %s\n", command);
    system(command);  // Execute the command.
    free(command);  
}

int main() {
    executeCommand("ls -l");  // Call the function with ls -l as argument.
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, `executeCommand` is a variadic function that takes a format string and any number of arguments for its placeholders. It creates a command string from these inputs using `vsnprintf()` and then uses the `system()` function to execute it as if you were typing into your shell. This will cause the program to list all files in the current directory.
']

