['Here's an example of C code implementing your requirements. Note that this example uses POSIX functions (for trimming) and assumes that you're working with a POSIX environment where `main` takes two parameters (argc, argv). 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

void trimTrailingWhitespace(char *str) {
    int index = strlen(str) - 1;
    while (index >= 0 && isspace((unsigned char) str[index])) {
        str[index--] = '\0';
    }
}

void trimNewLines(char *str) {
    char *src, *dst;
    for (src = dst = str; *src != '\0'; src++) {
        if (*src != '\n') {
            *dst++ = *src;
        }
    }
    *dst = '\0';
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Check that exactly one argument is provided.
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Please provide exactly one string as an argument.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // Get the user input string from command line arguments.
    char *inputStr = argv[1];

    // Trim trailing whitespace.
    trimTrailingWhitespace(inputStr);

    // Trim newlines.
    trimNewLines(inputStr);

    printf("Trimmed string: %s\n", inputStr);

    return 0;
}
```
This program will take the first command-line argument as an input, remove trailing whitespace and newlines from it (if any), and then print out the result. 

Please replace `#include <ctype.h>` with your platform's equivalent if not using a POSIX environment or if you want to use non-standard functions for trimming.
']

['Here's how you can write that in C code. This code assumes you are using UNIX-based system and using `getopt` to process command line arguments. 

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>
#include <unistd.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // Checking if exactly one argument is provided 
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Error: Exactly one argument is required.\n");
        return -1;
    }

    // Get the argument from command line and store it in a variable called input.
    char *input = argv[1];

    // Print the argument
    printf("Input Argument: %s\n", input);

    // Trim trailing whitespace 
    int len = strlen(input);
    while (len > 0 && isspace((unsigned char)input[len - 1])) {
        input[--len] = 0;
    }

    // Print the argument again after trimming
    printf("Trimmed Argument: %s\n", input);

    return 0;
}
```
This program works by checking if exactly one command line argument is given. If not, it will print an error message and exit with a status code of -1. If there's only one argument provided, the program stores this in `input` and prints it out. Afterwards, it trims trailing whitespace from the input using the `isspace` function and prints the trimmed result again.
']

['Here's a simple implementation of your requirements using the standard C library functions:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

void trim(char *str) {
    if (str == NULL) return;

    int len = strlen(str);

    while (isspace((unsigned char)*--str));
    str[++len] = 0;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s <argument>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    trim(argv[1]);
    printf("%s\n", argv[1]);

    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first check if the user supplied exactly one argument. If not, we print a usage message and exit with an error status. Then we trim any trailing whitespace from the command line argument, print it to stdout, and then return success. The `trim` function is used to remove all trailing whitespaces from a string. It works by starting at the end of the string and scanning backwards until it finds a non-whitespace character, then adding a null terminator one position beyond that.
']

['Here's a simple example of how you might implement this in C using libmicrohttpd, a lightweight HTTP server library in C that's often used for embedded systems or small web servers. Note that error checking and data validation are skipped here due to space constraints. Also, this assumes you have the libmicrohttpd library installed and includes it in your project.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <microhttpd.h>

int http_hello_page(void *cls, struct MHD_Connection *connection,
                    const char *url, 
                    const char *method, const char *version,
                    const char *upload_data, size_t *upload_data_size, void **con_cls) {
    static int dummy;
    struct MHD_Response *response;
    int ret;
    const char* username = NULL;

    // Read the request body into the username variable
    if (*upload_data_size != 0) {
        username = upload_data;
    }

    // Construct the response body 
    char outputbuf[1024];
    snprintf(outputbuf, sizeof(outputbuf), "<html><body>Hello, %s</body></html>", username ? username : "Guest");

    // Write the response body to the response stream
    response = MHD_create_response_from_buffer (strlen(outputbuf), 
                                               (void*) outputbuf,
                                               MHD_RESPMEM_MUST_COPY);
    ret = MHD_queue_response (connection, MHD_HTTP_OK, response);

    // Close the response stream
    MHD_destroy_response(response);

    return MHD_YES;
}
```
This code creates a simple HTTP server that responds with "Hello, [username]" when it receives a GET request to "/hello". If no username is provided in the request body, it will default to "Guest". Please note that this example doesn't handle POST requests or any other type of requests. Also, it doesn't properly free resources and isn't thread-safe for production use. It's a very basic demonstration of how you might implement a simple HTTP server in C using libmicrohttpd.
']

